<h1>Beats 101</h1>
<p class="lede">Rhythm is written as notes whose shapes tell you how long to hold them, measured against a steady beat.
This page starts from that beat, adds the note values one at a time, then puts them together into time signatures,
triplets, syncopation and swing — and ends with the drum beats most songs are built on.
Every example plays: press Play, count along out loud, then clap or tap it yourself.</p>

<p class="lede">Each example loops until you stop it. It counts you in for one bar, the metronome clicks the beat,
and the note sounding now lights up together with its count. Slow it down until you can clap it without thinking, then speed it up.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>The beat</h2>
<p>Under every song is a steady pulse — the beat you tap your foot to. Written music groups the beats into <b>bars</b>
(the vertical lines mark where one ends) and counts them from 1 at the start of each bar. In most music a bar holds four beats: 1 2 3 4, 1 2 3 4.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Four beats to a bar" note="One quarter note on every beat. Clap on each number." time="4/4" pattern="q q q q | q q q q" tempo=80}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Note values</h2>
<p>A note’s shape says how long it lasts, measured in beats. Each value is half the one above it:
one whole note lasts as long as two halves, four quarters, eight eighths or sixteen sixteenths.
Play the rows one after another — the metronome keeps the same beat while the notes get faster.</p>

<table class="values">
  <thead><tr><th>Note</th><th>Also called</th><th>Beats in 4/4</th><th>How it looks</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Whole</td><td>semibreve</td><td>4</td><td>hollow head, no stem</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Half</td><td>minim</td><td>2</td><td>hollow head with a stem</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter</td><td>crotchet</td><td>1</td><td>filled head with a stem</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Eighth</td><td>quaver</td><td>½</td><td>one flag, or one beam</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sixteenth</td><td>semiquaver</td><td>¼</td><td>two flags, or two beams</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Whole note" note="4 beats" time="4/4" pattern="w" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Half notes" note="2 beats each" time="4/4" pattern="h h" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Quarter notes" note="1 beat each" time="4/4" pattern="q q q q" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Eighth notes" note="2 to a beat" time="4/4" pattern="e e e e e e e e" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Sixteenth notes" note="4 to a beat" time="4/4" pattern="s s s s s s s s s s s s s s s s" tempo=80}}

<p>Eighths and sixteenths next to each other are joined by <b>beams</b> instead of flags — one beam for eighths, two for sixteenths —
grouped one beat at a time, so you can see where each beat starts. Between the beats, count “&amp;” halfway and “e” and “a” for the quarters in between:
<b>1</b> e &amp; a <b>2</b> e &amp; a.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Mixing values" note="Quarters, eighths and a half note in one bar." time="4/4" pattern="q e e h | e e e e q q" tempo=76}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Rests</h2>
<p>Every note value has a <b>rest</b> of the same length: a silence you count through. Keep counting during rests —
they are as much part of the rhythm as the notes. A whole rest hangs below the line; a half rest sits on top of it.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Half rest" note="2 beats of silence" time="4/4" pattern="h hr" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Quarter rests" note="1 beat each" time="4/4" pattern="q qr q qr" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Eighth rests" note="notes on the “&”" time="4/4" pattern="er e er e er e er e" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise compact=true name="Whole rest" note="a bar of silence" time="4/4" pattern="w | wr" tempo=80}}

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Count through the gaps" note="Say every number, even the silent ones." time="4/4" pattern="q qr q q | hr q qr" tempo=80}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Dots and ties</h2>
<p>A <b>dot</b> after a note makes it half as long again: a dotted half lasts 2 + 1 = 3 beats, a dotted quarter 1 + ½ = 1½.
A <b>tie</b> — a curve joining two notes — makes them one longer sound: play the first and hold it through the second.
Ties make lengths a single note can’t, and carry a note across a bar line.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Dotted half" note="Three beats, then one." time="4/4" pattern="h. q | h. q" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Dotted quarter and eighth" note="Long–short: the start of countless melodies." time="4/4" pattern="q. e q. e | q. e h" tempo=72}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="A tie" note="Sounds exactly like a dotted half and a quarter — written another way." time="4/4" pattern="h ~ q q | h ~ q q" tempo=80}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Time signatures</h2>
<p>The two numbers at the start are the <b>time signature</b>. The top number says how many beats are in a bar;
the bottom one says which note gets one beat — 4 for a quarter note, 8 for an eighth, 2 for a half.</p>

<ul class="howto">
  <li><b>4/4</b> — four quarter-note beats. Most pop, rock, hip-hop and folk. Also called common time, and written <b>C</b>.</li>
  <li><b>3/4</b> — three beats, strong–weak–weak: waltzes, many folk songs and ballads.</li>
  <li><b>2/4</b> — two beats: marches and polkas.</li>
  <li><b>6/8</b> — six eighths, felt as two beats of three: “<b>1</b> &amp; a <b>2</b> &amp; a”. Ballads, jigs, a lot of soul.</li>
  <li><b>12/8</b> — four beats of three: slow blues and doo-wop. <b>2/2</b> (cut time) — two half-note beats.
  <b>5/4</b> and <b>7/8</b> — uneven bars, felt as 3 + 2 or 2 + 2 + 3.</li>
</ul>

<p>In 2/4, 3/4 and 4/4 each beat splits in two — that’s <b>simple time</b>. In 6/8, 9/8 and 12/8 each beat splits in three — <b>compound time</b>:
the beat is a dotted quarter, eighths are beamed in threes, and the tempo counts dotted quarters.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="3/4: a waltz" note="Lean on the 1." time="3/4" pattern="h q | q q q | h. | q q q" tempo=108}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="2/4: a march" time="2/4" pattern="q q | e e q | q e e | h" tempo=104}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="6/8: two beats of three" note="The metronome clicks every eighth; the loud ones are the beats." time="6/8" pattern="e e e e e e | q e q e" tempo=60}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Triplets</h2>
<p>A <b>triplet</b> squeezes three notes into the time of two, marked with a 3. Eighth-note triplets split one beat into three even parts,
counted “<b>1</b> trip let <b>2</b> trip let”. Quarter-note triplets spread three notes evenly over two beats — they float against the beat, so count it carefully.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Eighths, then triplets" note="Two notes to a beat, then three." time="4/4" pattern="e e e e [e e e] [e e e]" tempo=72}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Quarter-note triplets" time="4/4" pattern="q q [q q q] | [q q q] h" tempo=72}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Syncopation</h2>
<p><b>Syncopation</b> puts notes between the beats, or holds them through a beat, so the accent lands where you don’t expect it.
The trick is to keep the beat steady in your head — count it out loud — and place the notes against it.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Off the beat" note="Notes only on the “&”." time="4/4" pattern="er e er e er e er e" tempo=84}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Short–long–short" note="The quarter note starts between beats 1 and 2." time="4/4" pattern="e q e q q | e q e h" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Pushing the beat" note="The eighth on the “&” of 2 is tied into beat 3: it arrives early and holds." time="4/4" pattern="q e e ~ q q | q e e ~ q q" tempo=80}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="The Charleston" note="Dotted quarter, eighth — then silence." time="4/4" pattern="q. e hr | q. e hr" tempo=96}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Swing</h2>
<p>In jazz, blues and shuffles, pairs of eighths aren’t played evenly: the first is long and the second short,
like the first and last notes of a triplet. Music usually writes plain eighths and marks the piece “swing”.
These two look the same and sound different.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Straight eighths" time="4/4" pattern="q e e q e e" tempo=100}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Swung eighths" note="Long–short, long–short." time="4/4" pattern="q e e q e e" tempo=100 swing=true}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Common drum beats</h2>
<p>Drum music uses the staff differently: each line or space is a drum, not a pitch. The hands play the <b>hi-hat</b>
(an × above the staff) and the <b>snare</b> (the middle space) with stems up; the right foot plays the <b>bass drum</b>
(the bottom space) with stems down. Almost every beat here puts the snare on 2 and 4 — the <b>backbeat</b>.</p>

<p class="hear">Hear each one:
  <button type="button" data-hit="hh">Hi-hat</button>
  <button type="button" data-hit="sn">Snare</button>
  <button type="button" data-hit="bd">Bass drum</button>
</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise name="Rock beat" note="Eighths on the hi-hat, bass drum on 1 and 3, snare on 2 and 4." time="4/4" tempo=96
    hands="e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh" feet="q:bd q_ q:bd q_"}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Rock beat, busier foot" note="An extra bass drum on the “&” of 3." time="4/4" tempo=96
    hands="e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh" feet="q:bd q_ e:bd e:bd q_"}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Four on the floor" note="Bass drum on every beat: disco, house, dance pop." time="4/4" tempo=120
    hands="e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh" feet="q:bd q:bd q:bd q:bd"}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Half-time" note="Snare only on 3: the same tempo feels twice as slow." time="4/4" tempo=96
    hands="e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh e:hh e:hh" feet="q:bd q_ h_"}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Shuffle" note="The rock beat, swung." time="4/4" tempo=92 swing=true
    hands="e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh+sn e:hh" feet="q:bd q_ q:bd q_"}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="Waltz" note="Bass drum on 1, snare and hi-hat on 2 and 3." time="3/4" tempo=108
    hands="q_ q:hh+sn q:hh+sn" feet="q:bd h_"}}
{{> rhythm-exercise name="6/8 ballad" note="Snare on the second big beat." time="6/8" tempo=50
    hands="e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh e:hh" feet="q:bd e_ q:sn e_"}}


