<h2>Practise it</h2>
<p>A little every day beats a lot once a week. Work down the list; each step builds on the one before.</p>

{{> rhythm-exercise random=true name="Sight-reading" note="Eight bars you haven’t seen before. While you play bars 5–8, bars 1–4 change; when you’re back on bar 1, bars 5–8 change. Read one line ahead." time="4/4" tempo=72}}
<ol class="notes">
  <li>Count every example out loud before you clap it — the number on each beat, “e &amp; a” in between. Counting is the skill; the clapping follows.</li>
  <li>Slow an example down until you can clap it right three times in a row, then raise the tempo by 5–10 and do it again.</li>
  <li>Once a rhythm is solid, switch the metronome off and see whether your beat stays steady without it.</li>
  <li>Take any chord you know and play it in each rhythm on this page — rhythm is what turns a chord into a song.</li>
  <li>For the drum beats, tap the hi-hat with your right hand, the snare with your left and the bass drum with your foot.
  Learn each limb on its own, slowly, then put them together.</li>
  <li>Test yourself: how many eighth notes fill a bar of 3/4? How long is a dotted half? Where is the second beat in 6/8?
  Does <b>q. e</b> sound different from a quarter tied to an eighth, then an eighth?
    <details><summary>Answers</summary>Six · three beats · on the fourth eighth note · no — they’re two ways to write the same rhythm.</details></li>
</ol>

<h2>Worth remembering</h2>
<ul class="notes">
  <li>The beat never stops: notes and rests are both measured against it.</li>
  <li>Each note value is half the one above it; a dot adds half again; a tie joins two notes into one.</li>
  <li>Top number: beats in a bar. Bottom number: which note is one beat.</li>
  <li>Simple time splits the beat in two, compound time (6/8, 12/8) in three; triplets borrow a three-way split inside simple time.</li>
  <li>On drums, the backbeat — snare on 2 and 4 — is what makes a beat sound like rock and pop.</li>
</ul>

